# Lab 6: Docker environment variables and resource management

In this lab I containerized a Python app, passed environment variables in 3 different ways, and limited the container resources.

App source: https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/PythonApp.git

## Clone the code

```bash
git clone https://github.com/Ibrahim-Adel15/PythonApp.git
cd PythonApp
```
## ## Step 1: Create Dockerfile
FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt
COPY . .
EXPOSE 5000
CMD ["python", "app.py"]


## Step 2: Build the image

```bash
docker build -t ivolve1 .
```

## Step 3: Run container1 with env variables and limits

```bash
docker run -d --name container1 -p 5000:5000 \
  -e APP_NAME=iVolveApp1 -e APP_ENV=stg \
  --cpus=1 --memory=256m \
  ivolve1
```

## Step 4: Verify the app

```bash
curl http://localhost:5000
```

## Step 5: Verify the environment variables inside the container

```bash
docker exec container1 env | grep APP_
```

## Step 6: Check the resource usage

```bash
docker stats container1
```
doc
To confirm the limits are applied:

```bash
docker inspect container
```

## Step 7: Use an env file

Create `env.txt`:

```
APP_NAME=iVolveApp2
APP_ENV=prod
```

Port 5000 is already used by container1, so I removed it first:

```bash
docker rm -f container1

docker run -d --name container2 -p 5000:5000 --env-file env.txt ivolve1
docker inspect container2
```

## Step 8: Hardcode the variables in the Dockerfile


```dockerfile
ENV APP_NAME=iVolveApp3
ENV APP_ENV=dev
```

Build a new image:

```bash
docker build -t ivolve2 .
```

Remove container2 and run it again from the new image:

```bash
docker rm -f container2

docker run -d --name container2 -p 5000:5000 ivolve2
curl http://localhost:5000
docker inspect container2 
```

The output should show `APP_NAME=iVolveApp3` and `APP_ENV=dev`.



